# ── roster-completeness round 2: codex BLOCKERs (all reproduced) ─────
# B1 — tmux session targets PREFIX-MATCH. Reproduced live: session 'foobar' held
# @ae_agents_status; running cleanup for the NON-EXISTENT session 'foo'
# (set-option -w -t 'foo:0' -u ...) WIPED foobar's options. A user with sessions 'ae' and
# 'aedev' would lose aedev's bar when ae's watchdog stops. PRE-EXISTING for the session
# options; this slice widened it to per-window ones.
#
# THE FIX IS NOT tmux's '=' PREFIX. Measured on tmux 3.4: '=name' works for has-session
# but FAILS for show-options/set-option ("no such session: =name") and yields nothing
# from display-message. An earlier attempt to anchor with '=' LOOKED like it fixed the
# clobber — the neighbour survived — but only because EVERY write silently failed; it
# blanked the bar and 14 integration tests caught what my source pins could not. Testing
# that a fix prevents the bad outcome is not testing that it preserves the good one.
#
# And the LOOKUP prefix-matches too: `display-message -t foo '#{session_id}'` returns
# foobar's id when foo does not exist — so resolving a name through any tmux target
# reintroduces the bug. Match the name as an exact bash string over a list-sessions dump;
# the resulting '$N' id is unique and stable, exactly like the '@N' window ids.
_ls_dump="$(printf '%s\n' '$0 foobar' '$1 ae' '$2 aedev' '$3 has spaces')"
assert_eq "session-id: exact name match wins (never a prefix)" '$2' "$(printf '%s' "$_ls_dump" | _roster_session_id aedev)"
assert_eq "session-id: 'ae' resolves to ae, NOT to aedev/foobar" '$1' "$(printf '%s' "$_ls_dump" | _roster_session_id ae)"
